ccsd is putting plans in place to ensure  that both teachers and students are safe  when returning to school  thanks for joining us i'm denise feldas  and i'm brian loftis in a little more  than two weeks  pre-k right through third grade students  will transition to a hybrid learning  model  tonight the district reached agreements  with local unions again focused on staff  and student safety  sasha loftis has been combing through  all of those details and joins us now  from the newsroom with more  sasha  denise and brian this nearly 3 million  dollar agreement  will address safety concerns across the  district and provide things like masks  and disinfectant  for staff all across different campuses  as they start to reopen  in preparation for that hybrid learning  on march  1st now this plan includes personal  protective equipment for  every employee including face coverings  and disinfectant  each employee will also be required to  undergo daily symptom  screening and mandatory covid19 testing  every month  the new agreement also provides  additional compensation for any  any extra duties performed for some  staff if an employee  does test positive they have the option  to work from home if possible  and can return in person after a  negative test  now there were more than 100 pages of  public comments  during this meeting now a lot asks for  sports to be brought back for students  now governor sisselak actually mentioned  that as well today  saying that school bringing kids back to  campus is really the main priority there  and then they will address sports and  that coming back afterward  for now reporting live in the newsroom  tonight sasha loftis 8 news now
 

